## **10A. Introduction** {.unlisted}

The oak ambrosia beetle (OAB), *Platypus quercivorus* Murayama (Coleoptera: Platypodidae), is a destructive forest pest distributed across India, Indonesia, Japan, and Papua New Guinea (`\hyperref[ref-davis-2005]{Davis et al. 2005}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-davis-2005">Davis et al. 2005</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2011]{USDA-APHIS 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2011">USDA-APHIS 2011</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-eppo-2020]{EPPO 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-eppo-2020">EPPO 2020</a>`{=html}). The beetle vectors its symbiotic fungal pathogen, *Raffaelea quercivora* Kubono & Shin-Ito, which causes Japanese oak wilt disease (JOW) by disrupting tracheary function and can kill both healthy and stressed trees (`\hyperref[ref-kubono-ito-2002]{Kubono and Ito 2002}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kubono-ito-2002">Kubono and Ito 2002</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-kamata-2002]{Kamata et al. 2002}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kamata-2002">Kamata et al. 2002</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-davis-2005]{Davis et al. 2005}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-davis-2005">Davis et al. 2005</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-kobayashi-ueda-2005]{Kobayashi and Ueda 2005}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kobayashi-ueda-2005">Kobayashi and Ueda 2005</a>`{=html}). Suitable host plants for reproduction include species of *Quercus* as well as other members of the Fagaceae, including chestnut (*Castanea* spp.), chinquapin (*Castanopsis* spp.), and stone oaks (*Lithocarpus* spp.), although OAB may also attack non-host tree species near areas experiencing mass infestation (`\hyperref[ref-davis-2005]{Davis et al. 2005}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-davis-2005">Davis et al. 2005</a>`{=html}). The beetle–fungus complex has caused extensive oak mortality in Japan, where OAB preferentially attacks larger trees with higher water content, making abandoned oak coppice forests particularly vulnerable to infestation (`\hyperref[ref-kobayashi-ueda-2005]{Kobayashi and Ueda 2005}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kobayashi-ueda-2005">Kobayashi and Ueda 2005</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-nakajima-2019]{Nakajima 2019}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-nakajima-2019">Nakajima 2019</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-eppo-2020]{EPPO 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-eppo-2020">EPPO 2020</a>`{=html}). If introduced to the United States, OAB and JOW could pose substantial economic, environmental, and social risks because of the potential for mortality of susceptible oak and other *Fagaceae* species. Although *R. quercivora* has only been reported from Japan, the distribution of OAB across multiple countries in Asia and Oceania highlights the potential for movement of the beetle through international trade and other pathways (`\hyperref[ref-davis-2005]{Davis et al. 2005}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-davis-2005">Davis et al. 2005</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2011]{USDA-APHIS 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2011">USDA-APHIS 2011</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-eppo-2020]{EPPO 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-eppo-2020">EPPO 2020</a>`{=html}).

Several studies have predicted suitable conditions for OAB across much of the United States, although the predicted range varies among studies (`\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2011]{USDA-APHIS 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2011">USDA-APHIS 2011</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-barker-coop-2020]{Barker and Coop 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-coop-2020">Barker and Coop 2020</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-takeuchi-2023]{Takeuchi et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-takeuchi-2023">Takeuchi et al. 2023</a>`{=html}). Suitable conditions have been predicted across most of the country, including much of the Pacific Coast, Puerto Rico, and Hawaii, with the northern Midwest generally representing areas of lower suitability (`\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2011]{USDA-APHIS 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2011">USDA-APHIS 2011</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-barker-coop-2020]{Barker and Coop 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-coop-2020">Barker and Coop 2020</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-takeuchi-2023]{Takeuchi et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-takeuchi-2023">Takeuchi et al. 2023</a>`{=html}). Areas with abundant oak resources would therefore be expected to face the greatest risk of establishment and impact. OAB could potentially complete one to three generations annually in the United States, compared with generally one generation per year in Japan, although a second generation may occasionally occur (`\hyperref[ref-kamata-2002]{Kamata et al. 2002}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kamata-2002">Kamata et al. 2002</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-barker-coop-2020]{Barker and Coop 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-coop-2020">Barker and Coop 2020</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-takeuchi-2023]{Takeuchi et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-takeuchi-2023">Takeuchi et al. 2023</a>`{=html}). When development is incomplete before winter, larvae overwinter as fifth instars (`\hyperref[ref-kinuura-1995]{Kinuura 1995}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kinuura-1995">Kinuura 1995</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-kinuura-2002]{Kinuura 2002}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kinuura-2002">Kinuura 2002</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2011]{USDA-APHIS 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2011">USDA-APHIS 2011</a>`{=html}). OAB is also capable of active flight and exhibits greater flight activity under humid conditions, while warmer temperatures during the rainy season may further increase dispersal activity (`\hyperref[ref-eppo-2020]{EPPO 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-eppo-2020">EPPO 2020</a>`{=html}; `\hyperref[ref-kobayashi-2020]{Kobayashi 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kobayashi-2020">Kobayashi 2020</a>`{=html}).

A model for OAB developed for the Degree-Day, establishment Risk, and Phenological event mapping system (DDRP) is operationalized to produce near real-time forecasts for the contiguous United States (CONUS) at <https://uspest.org/DDRP>. The phenology model applies a lower developmental threshold of 11.1 ºC and upper threshold of 38 ºC for all life stages (egg, larva, pupa, and adult) and assumes the larva is the overwintering stage. The climatic suitability model applies a lower cold stress threshold and upper heat stress threshold of −10 °C and 38 °C, respectively. The full set of model parameters for OAB, including degree-day requirements for individual life stages and climate stress limits, are presented in @tbl-oab-parameters. Details on model parameterization are available at USPest.org (`\hyperref[ref-barker-coop-2020]{Barker and Coop 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-coop-2020">Barker and Coop 2020</a>`{=html}).

## **10B. Results** {.unlisted}

### **i. Phenology**

#### **Entire period: 1981--2025**

Over 1981–2025, predicted adult emergence and egg hatch generally shifted earlier across much of the southern and central United States, with later events in portions of the northern interior (@fig-oab-emergence, @fig-oab-egg-hatch). Significant changes were concentrated in the eastern and central United States and in scattered western areas.


